If no `*` is found, `end` becomes `buf` (the loop exits when `buf < end` is false, i.e., `end == buf`). Then `if ('*' != *end)` checks `*buf`. If `buf[0]` is not `*`, returns false. Fine.

But if `*` is found at `endp - 1` (the last byte), then `end[1]` = `*endp` and `end[2]` = `*(endp+1)`. Since `endp = lexer->inbufptr` which is one past the last byte read, `end[1]` reads the byte at `inbufptr` and `end[2]` reads at `inbufptr + 1`. These are within the 18433-byte array but past the actual data. Not a stack overflow.

